    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In recent years, the idea of an artificial intelligence (AI) arms race between the United States (U.S.) and China has gained traction in policy circles. Correspondingly, many analysts have sought to predict its trajectory. Despite their attempts to objectively and quantitatively assess this competition, however, they come to drastically opposing conclusions. This thesis probes this phenomenon, asking why analysts, despite relying on the same data, come to divergent findings. In particular, it explores the understudied effects of social factors on assessing security-relevant technology, focusing on how the sociocultural and political contexts in which analysts assess the AI race introduce subjectivity into their interpretations and conclusions. Through thematic analysis of fifty- four in-depth, semi-structured interviews of AI experts across the U.S.’ and China’s ecosystems, this thesis identifies five narratives through which subject matter experts explain the question of relative AI capabilities. Rooted respectively in techno-optimism, skepticism of imitative technology development, confidence in China’s state-driven approach, path dependency, and doubt about the appropriateness of an “AI race” metaphor, they express different sets of core values and interests, which lead to different sets of criteria for predicting present and future AI leadership. Adherence to these narratives falls predominantly along the lines of nationality and degree of affiliation with the government. For example, while individuals at entities connected to the U.S. government are more likely to believe that the U.S. trails China in AI development, similar groups in China tend to assert that the opposite is the case. Such findings add to the existing body of work on “interpretative flexibility,” the fact that technological artifacts hold different meanings and interpretations for different social groups. They may also offer policymakers insights into the interplay of technology and the state, especially when it comes to the influence of government-driven narratives on public perceptions today.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
